<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Combinations in Real Life 1 - Sampling</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) A doctor selects \({@pr1@}\) patients from a group of \({@pn1@}\) volunteers to test a new vitamin. How many different groups can be formed?</p>
<p>Number of groups = [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>
<p>(b) A factory inspector picks 3 smartphones at random from a box of \({@pn2@}\) to check for defects. How many different samples can the inspector choose?</p>
<p>Number of samples = [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>All the selected patients receive the same vitamin, so order does not matter. This is \(_{ {@pn1@} }C_{ {@pr1@} }\):</p>
<p>\(_{ {@pn1@} }C_{ {@pr1@} } = \dfrac{ {@pn1@}!}{ {@pr1@}!\,{@pn1-pr1@}!} = {@ta1@}\)</p>
<p>For part (b), testing phones A, B, C is the same as testing C, B, A, so this is \(_{ {@pn2@} }C_3\):</p>
<p>\(_{ {@pn2@} }C_3 = \dfrac{ {@pn2@} \times {@pn2-1@} \times {@pn2-2@} }{3 \times 2 \times 1} = {@ta2@}\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
